  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nCurrently have a deep upper trough centered over southern NV and \nextending through the Desert Southwest. Meanwhile an upper ridge \nremains over the Gulf Coast of TX/LA. At the surface, strong cold \nfront currently stretches from near Kansas City to near Topeka \nand near Hutchinson, with a loosely defined dryline extending \nsouth from around Dodge City.\n\nA few elevated storms develop early this afternoon near Dodge City \nas better upper diffluence starts to move-in along with a surge of \nhigher 850mb dew points. Still  looking for additional development \nlater this afternoon along the front as it pushes south with \nactivity expanding in coverage early this evening as low level jet \nramps-up and enhancing lift along and especially north of the \nsurface front. As the evening progresses, right entrance region of \nupper jet will also become more defined across central KS further \nenhancing lift and associated storm coverage. Unseasonably high PW \nvalues around 2 inches will allow any of the stronger showers \nor storms to produce high rainfall rates and potential flooding \ntonight into early Wed morning. In addition, even elevated \nstorms will have over 1500J/kg of CAPE to work with along with \n35-40kts of effective shear which should be enough to get some \nhail. \n\nBy late Wed morning, deep upper low will be tracking across the Four \nCorners region with strong upper diffluence remaining over the area \nalong with a broad area of mid level theta-e advection. This will \nkeep at least some sct showers and storms around on Wed. We are \nstill expecting another round of more concentrated showers and \nstorms Wed evening through early Thu morning as another jet max \nlifts northeast out of the TX Panhandle with the main response \nanother low level jet surging into southern KS. This setup should \nbring another round of heavy rain Wed night, with the Flint Hills \ninto southeast KS having the higher chances for heavy rain and \nflooding. \n\nNorthern stream shortwave is expected to track out of Southern \nSaskatchewan across the Northern Plains Wed night into Thu morning \nand into the Mid/Upper Mississippi Valley by Thu evening. This will \nhelp to quickly push the upper impulse east and at the same time \nshear it out. So by late Thu afternoon, only southeast KS will still \nbe seeing rainfall chances with the cold front quickly surging south \nacross the Southern Plains. Surface high pressure will quickly slide \nacross the Plains on Fri into Fri night providing widespread fall \ntemps. Highs on Fri are only expected to reach the upper 50s to\naround 60 degrees with widespread lows in the 30s Sat morning.\nThis will lead to fairly widespread frost across central KS Sat\nmorning. We will quickly get back into some warm advection for \nSun into Mon as the surface high shifts east.",
